WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.632
The teaching database combines healthcare-style and retail-style examples.

00:00:04.632 --> 00:00:07.732
Patients gives us messy contact text.

00:00:07.732 --> 00:00:12.022
Admissions gives us timestamps for elapsed-time calculations.

00:00:12.022 --> 00:00:15.617
Products helps with substring and replacement examples.

00:00:15.617 --> 00:00:20.117
Orders gives us timestamps and numeric measures for monthly summaries.

00:00:20.117 --> 00:00:26.412
Staging dates contains raw text dates in different formats so we can practice safe parsing.

00:00:26.412 --> 00:00:31.947
Connect this idea to an operations team aligning timestamps across locations.

00:00:31.947 --> 00:00:37.767
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:37.767 --> 00:00:45.437
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:00:45.437 --> 00:00:50.932
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:00:50.932 --> 00:00:56.838
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
